(text "SPI (Serial Peripheral Interface) nets, from the code:\n  nRF52 side: upstream/gcwireless src/rx.cpp @ 576878d lines 72-76: nrf_spim_pins_set(NRF_SPIM3,\n    PIN_020 /*SCK*/, PIN_022 /*MOSI*/, PIN_024 /*MISO*/). With build-support/gcwireless/pins.h (ours),\n    PIN_020 = P0.20, PIN_022 = P0.22, PIN_024 = P0.24 (D3, D4, D5 on the official image).\n  RP2040 side: upstream/gcwireless_bridge @ 5c31147 src/main.cpp lines 85-90 (peripheral; upstream calls it slave, on the SDK default\n    SPI0 pins: SCK GP18, RX GP16, TX GP19, CSn GP17; values from pico.h in Pico SDK 2.1.1).\n  So SCK P0.20 -> GP18, COPI P0.22 -> GP16 (the Pico's RX), CIPO P0.24 <- GP19 (the Pico's TX).\n  Direction wording (COPI = controller out, peripheral in; CIPO = the reverse; older names MOSI/MISO) is our reading of which side is\n    the controller; the code does not say it outright.\nJoybus DATA: bridge GP27 (src/main.cpp:10) to GameCube plug pin 2 (int03 numbering).\nTBD: chip select. rx.cpp lines 66-71 has the nRF CSN setup (PIN_002) commented out, so no nRF pin is chosen;\n  GP17 (CSn) is drawn dangling as SPI_CSN_TBD.\nTBD: how the receiver is powered (GC +5V and 3.43V are labelled only); the code is silent.\nNote the bridge currently copies only byte 0 of the SPI data (main.cpp line 104), and reads 10 bytes." (exclude_from_sim no) (at 12.000 16.000 0) (effects (font (size 1.9 1.9)) (justify left top)) (uuid "48ddb41e-e3d6-542a-bbde-9ef83d481b20"))
(embedded_fonts no)
